AUDIO: 'This is OpenWrt. Linux operating system that frees routers. The box from your provider is a real computer with closed proprietary firmware that cannot be modified. OpenWrt replaces it with a'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'full Linux system with a package manager, ad blocking, WireGuard, VLANs, and bandwidth control on over 2,200 devices, including those that have been abandoned by manufacturers'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '. The project has been in development for over two decades and has just released its largest release in recent years . Let\'s free up the router. Let\'s start with the number that defines this project. Over 2,200'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'supported devices in the current stable series, with over 180 added this cycle alone. These are routers, access points, and single-board computers from all the manufacturers you know and dozens of others'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you haven\'t heard of. They are all listed in the legendary equipment table, where you enter your model and in 10 seconds you will find out if your router has a path to freedom. The table doesn\'t just say "yes" or "no." It contains links to'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'firmware instructions, known issues, and versions that support the device . OpenWrt appeared in 2004 based on the source code that Linksys was forced to release for the WRT54G router. This is'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'perhaps the most important case of GPL enforcement in the history of consumer hardware. After 22 years, it became a volunteer institution. Thousands of contributors, lead'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
